/// The screen through the compositor (issue 086). `init` gives `compositor` a service endpoint and the shell a client of
/// it in SLOT_DISPLAY (kernel issue 151); the shell's `screenshot` uses it, and the video gateway lights the camera
/// mark with it (1.1); `link` and the Effector gateway light the marks of who can reach the machine from outside (1.2);
/// a display driver offers it a second screen, which it mirrors the first screen into (1.3, 213-KRN-0272).
package mind:display@1.3.0;

interface display {
    /// Why a capture failed: no task has a screen in front yet, or there is no memory for the copy; `denied`: a
    /// `remote` from a client with no mark of its own (1.2), or a second screen from a client not badged
    /// `BADGE_SCREEN` (1.3); `bad-screen`: a second screen whose shape the lent pixels do not hold, or whose format or
    /// size the compositor does not draw (1.3).
    enum error { no-screen, no-memory, denied, bad-screen }
    /// The screen's size in pixels and its stride (pixels per row, at least the width).
    record mode { width: u32, height: u32, stride: u32 }

    mode: func() -> mode;
    /// What is on the screen now (the screen of the task in front): a sealed read-only copy (SHARE_RO) of
    /// `stride * height` pixels `0x00RRGGBB`, row after row.
    capture: func() -> result<own<memory>, error>;
    /// A camera stream is open: the compositor shows the camera mark for 1.5 s after the last call. The video gateway
    /// calls it while any stream is open (issue 158, 1.1); nothing turns the mark off sooner.
    camera: func();
    /// What a remote path may do now: nothing, as it is not connected; read; or change the machine.
    enum remote-state { unconnected, reading, changing }
    /// The caller's remote mark (1.2, 800-KRN-0156), shown for 3 s after the last call, grey, yellow or red for its
    /// state: a ring with a dot for a client badged `BADGE_REMOTE_LINK` (link's), a filled circle for one badged
    /// `BADGE_REMOTE_EFFECTOR` (the Effector gateway's), both in `mind::display`. Other clients get `denied`.
    remote: func(state: remote-state) -> result<_, error>;

    /// 1.3 (213-KRN-0272): a second screen's pixels as they lie in memory: `bgr`, each pixel a 32-bit word
    /// `0x00RRGGBB` (blue first in memory, as UEFI's `PixelBlueGreenRedReserved8BitPerColor` and the shadow's own);
    /// `rgb`, red first.
    enum pixel-format { bgr, rgb }
    /// A display driver, badged `BADGE_SCREEN` by init (`mind::display`), offers a second screen it drives: its width and
    /// height in pixels, its pitch in bytes from one row to the next (at least `width * 4`, a multiple of 4), its pixel
    /// format, and its pixels, a register range it lends (MMIO: the card's frame memory, mapped write-combining on x86 when it
    /// lies in the boot framebuffer's BAR) that holds at least `pitch * height` bytes. A lease: the driver keeps its range,
    /// and calls `screen-gone` before it revokes the copy or lets the range go. From the next frame the
    /// compositor mirrors the first screen into it, scaled to fit, centred, the rest black, with the marks the first
    /// screen shows (capture, camera, remote) drawn on it too. Offering again replaces the screen before: a monitor
    /// plugged in anew, or another mode. Nothing is drawn there before the call returns; the driver shows the pixels
    /// (unblanks its pipe) when it wants.
    offer-screen: func(width: u16, height: u16, pitch: u32, format: pixel-format, pixels: borrow<mmio>) -> result<_, error>;
    /// The second screen is gone (its monitor unplugged, its pipe off): the compositor stops drawing into it and lets
    /// its mapping go before it answers.
    screen-gone: func() -> result<_, error>;
}
